        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-05-04T13:54:33Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Just realized: FEADDR is a 32-bit register, right? While we are just allowing the host to pass len\u003d8? So, what happens then? It may just overwrite the next 32-bit register, i.e. FEUADDR in this case? (And of course similarly, FEDATA would overwrite FEADDR, and so on?)\n\nSo we should validate `len` (per register) whenever using iommu_direct_mmio_write() or iommu_direct_mmio_read() for the given register?\n\nMaybe that would even obliterate the need for those iommu_direct_mmio_write() and iommu_direct_mmio_read() wrappers as such, since basically all they do is validate that `len` is either 4 or 8 (but allowing both), while we need to allow either 4 only or 8 only, per register, so upon that validation we could just directly use writel()/writeq()/readl()/readq() here instead.",
      "revId": "94db379928e0f81788e50e48dc96c6b4384ff5bd",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1c27f637_c3f40674",
        "filename": "drivers/iommu/intel/pkvm/iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 12
      },
      "lineNbr": 529,
      "author": {
        "id": 3702829
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-05-04T21:01:06Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Just realized: FEADDR is a 32-bit register, right? While we are just allowing the host to pass len\u003d8? So, what happens then? It may just overwrite the next 32-bit register, i.e. FEUADDR in this case? (And of course similarly, FEDATA would overwrite FEADDR, and so on?)\n\nGood catch, malicious host can pass 8 for u32 regs and may overwrite registers. Just did a quick test and IOMMU does write to adjacent registers.\n\n\u003e Maybe that would even obliterate the need for those iommu_direct_mmio_write() and iommu_direct_mmio_read() wrappers as such, since basically all they do is validate that len is either 4 or 8 (but allowing both), while we need to allow either 4 only or 8 only, per register, so upon that validation we could just directly use writel()/writeq()/readl()/readq() here instead.\n\nNice idea, will rework and get the wrappers removed",
      "parentUuid": "d9bfe9dc_5b1dd8be",
      "revId": "94db379928e0f81788e50e48dc96c6b4384ff5bd",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
